---
description: React/TypeScript conventions for MyTravels' web app
globs: src/web/src/**/*.{ts,tsx}
alwaysApply: false
---

- oxlint (`src/web/.oxlintrc.json`) already enforces `react/rules-of-hooks` and
  `react/only-export-components` — don't restate those.

**Components**
- Declare components as named `function` declarations exported inline
  (`export function MapView(...) { ... }`), not `const X = () => {}` or
  `React.FC<Props>`. See `MapView.tsx`, `PoiDialog.tsx`,
  `LocationSearchDialog.tsx`, `UploadButton.tsx`, `Spinner.tsx`.
- `App.tsx` is the one exception: it's declared as `function App()` but
  exported via `export default App;` at the bottom of the file, because
  `main.tsx` imports it as a default. This default export is reserved for
  the app root — every other component/function in `src/web/src` uses a
  named export.

**Props**
- Name the props interface `<ComponentName>Props` (`MapViewProps`,
  `PoiDialogProps`, `UploadButtonProps`, `SpinnerProps`,
  `LocationSearchDialogProps`, `PostmarkGlyphProps`).
- Declare the interface directly above the component in the same file —
  props types don't live in a separate types file.
- Destructure props in the function signature (`function Spinner({
  className = "h-4 w-4" }: SpinnerProps)`), including defaults for optional
  props, rather than accessing `props.x` in the body.

**Multi-state UI**
- Model a UI element's lifecycle as a single `useState` typed with a union,
  not separate booleans (`loading`/`error`/`data` flags). For richer states
  that carry data, use a discriminated union keyed on `status`, e.g.
  `SearchState` (`LocationSearchDialog.tsx`) and `ImageState`/`DescribeState`
  (`PoiDialog.tsx`): `{ status: "loading" } | { status: "loaded"; src: string
  } | { status: "error" }`. For simple cases with no associated data, a plain
  string-literal union is enough, e.g. `UploadState = 'idle' | 'uploading' |
  'error'` (`UploadButton.tsx`).
- Name the type `<Thing>State` and switch on `.status` in JSX with
  `state.status === "..."` checks rather than nested ternaries.

**Imports**
- `verbatimModuleSyntax` is on in `tsconfig.app.json`, so type-only imports
  must use `import type`. Write them as their own statement, separate from
  any value import of the same module — e.g. in `App.tsx`:
  `import type { PointOfInterest } from './api/types';` on its own line,
  even though `hasCoordinates` is imported as a value from the same
  `./api/types` module in the next line. Don't mix `import { type X, y }`.
- Order imports external-package-first, then relative imports.

**Handlers/callbacks**
- Prop callbacks are named `onX` (`onSelect`, `onClose`, `onUploaded`).
- The component-local function that implements a callback is named
  `handleX` (`handleUploaded`, `handleFileChange`, `handlePlaceSelected`,
  `handleDescribe`, `handlePointerDown`).
- Named handlers and other component-local helpers are `function`
  declarations (`function handleFileChange(e) {...}`), not
  `const handleFileChange = (e) => {...}`. Reserve inline arrow functions
  for trivial one-line callbacks passed directly to a prop (`onClick={() =>
  setSelectedPoi(null)}`).

**Constants**
- Config values and magic numbers are `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`, declared at
  module top level above the component/function that uses them (not inline,
  not in a shared constants file). Suffix the unit in the name where one
  applies: `POLL_INTERVAL_MS`, `POLL_MAX_ATTEMPTS` (`App.tsx`),
  `DEBOUNCE_MS`, `MIN_QUERY_LENGTH` (`LocationSearchDialog.tsx`),
  `DEFAULT_ZOOM`, `FOCUSED_ZOOM` (`MapView.tsx`), `PIN_SIZE`
  (`leafletIcon.ts`).

**API calls**
- All API calls live in `src/api/client.ts` as plain exported `async`
  functions (or functions returning a `Promise`) — one function per
  endpoint, not hooks. Response shapes are typed from `src/api/types.ts`.
- Every function that issues a `fetch` takes an optional trailing
  `signal?: AbortSignal` parameter and forwards it to `fetch`.
- Error handling is centralized in two helpers in `client.ts`:
  `throwForStatus` (throws `Error("{status} {statusText}: {body}")` on a
  non-ok response) and `unwrap<T>` (calls `throwForStatus` then parses
  JSON). Callers don't re-implement status checks; they catch the thrown
  `Error` and translate it into local UI state.

**Fetch cancellation in effects**
- Effects that call the API create an `AbortController`, pass
  `controller.signal` into the API call, and return `() =>
  controller.abort()` as the cleanup function. In the `.catch`, ignore the
  abort by checking `if (err.name !== "AbortError")` before setting error
  state. See `LocationSearchDialog.tsx` and `PoiDialog.tsx`.

**Comments**
- Comments are rare and only explain non-obvious *why*, never restate what
  the code does. Used for: a workaround or gotcha (`client.ts`: "The API
  returns the base64 payload as text/plain, not JSON."; `telemetry.ts`:
  the `propagateTraceHeaderCorsUrls` regex-vs-string-match note), or a
  short JSDoc line above an exported function/type/component clarifying
  intent (`client.ts`'s `uploadPointOfInterestImage*` functions,
  `PostmarkGlyph`, `defaultIcon`, `UploadMode`).

**Styling**
- Tailwind utility classes inline in `className`, no CSS modules or
  styled-components. Design tokens (colors, fonts) are defined once via
  `@theme` in `index.css` and consumed as utility classes (`bg-paper`,
  `text-ink`, `border-brass/40`), not as inline styles or JS constants.
- Dark mode is done by pairing each color utility with a `dark:` variant on
  the same element (`text-ink dark:text-bone`), driven by
  `prefers-color-scheme` (`:root { color-scheme: light dark }` in
  `index.css`) — there's no class-based theme toggle or context.

**File naming**
- A file whose module exports one component is named for that component in
  PascalCase (`MapView.tsx`, `PoiDialog.tsx`, `UploadButton.tsx`,
  `Spinner.tsx`, `LocationSearchDialog.tsx`).
- A file that exports plain functions/values, or a category of small
  components, is named in camelCase (`client.ts`, `types.ts`,
  `leafletIcon.ts`, `telemetry.ts`, `icons.tsx`).

**State management**
- No external state library. State is local `useState`/`useRef`; when more
  than one component needs it, it's lifted to the closest common ancestor
  and passed down as props (`App.tsx` owns `pois` and `selectedPoi`, passed
  into `MapView` and `PoiDialog`) rather than reached for via context.
